---
// src/pages/what-is-yini-and-why.astro
import CONFIG from '../config/conf'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

/*
    NOTE: This page is intended for very first time users:
    - That has Zero context of what YINI is.
    - Even more focused than the front page (where users probably has been forwarden from somewhere else).

    1. What YINI is
    2. Why it exists
    3. Why they should care
    4. How to try it in under 60 seconds
 */
---

<BaseLayout
    headTitle="What is YINI and Why?"
    headDescription="YINI is a human-friendly, text-based configuration format — less verbose than JSON, simpler than YAML, and more expressive than INI."
>
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-2 text-center">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-5xl">
            What is YINI and Why?
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base md:text-lg">
            YINI is a human-friendly, text-based configuration format — less
            verbose than JSON, simpler than YAML, and more expressive than INI.
        </p>
        <p>
            A calm, predictable way to write configuration and structured data
            for real-world projects.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
            <a href="/get-started" class="btn btn-primary"> Get Started </a>

            <!-- !!Temp disabled
            <a href="/playground" class="btn btn-outline"> Open Playground </a>
            -->

            <a href="/yini-code-examples" class="inline-block underline">
                View Examples
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="why-yini pb-4">
        <h2>❓ What is YINI?</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>YINI</strong> is a text-based data format designed for humans
            first.
        </p>
        It combines:
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                the simplicity of <strong>INI</strong>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                the structure of <strong>JSON</strong>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                the flexibility people <i>want</i> from <strong>YAML</strong>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">🧯 Why does YINI exist?</h2>
        Most config formats fail as projects grow.
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>INI</strong> becomes flat and unstructured
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>JSON</strong> becomes noisy and hard to edit
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>YAML</strong> becomes fragile and unpredictable
            </li>
        </ul>
        YINI was created to fix that.
    </section>

    <section class="pb-4">
        <h2>✨ What makes YINI different?</h2>
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Clear structure without indentation rules</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">No fragile whitespace semantics.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Readable nesting that scales</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">
                    Files remain easy to scan even when large.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Predictable parsing</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">Defined by a formal grammar and spec.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Human-first design</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">Comments, consistency, minimal noise.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Balanced design</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">
                    More structure than INI, less noise than JSON, fewer edge
                    cases than YAML.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">💡 What can I do with YINI?</h2>
        Build, convert, and consume structured configuration and data using simple
        tools.
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">🛠 Convert YINI → JSON via CLI.</li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                📦 Load YINI into your app as native objects.
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                🧩 Use YINI for configuration, data files, pipelines, and
                tooling.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">⚖️ Quick comparison</h2>
        <h3>"YAML foot-gun" vs YINI predictability (tabs/indentation)</h3>
        <h3>YAML:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yaml">{`# One stray indent changes the structure
app:
  name: My App
  server:
    host: 127.0.0.1
    port: 8080
   allowedOrigins:   # <- off by one indent (hard to spot)
    - https://myapp.com
    - "http://localhost:3000"`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            <strong
                >YAML uses indentation to define structure; YINI uses explicit
                section markers.</strong
            >
            This makes large configs easier to scan and harder to break accidentally.
        </p>

        <h3>YINI:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yini">{`# Structure is defined by markers; indentation is optional
^ App
name = "My App"

^^ Server
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 8080
allowedOrigins = ["https://myapp.com", "http://localhost:3000"]
`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            <strong>Point:</strong> In YINI the hierarchy is signaled by markers,
            not whitespace.
        </p>

        <div style="overflow-x:auto">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>YINI</th>
                        <th>INI</th>
                        <th>JSON</th>
                        <th>YAML</th>
                        <th>TOML</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>

                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Human-friendly by default</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Readability at scale</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Predictable parsing</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Formal grammar / spec</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Nested structure</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Comments</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Minimal syntax noise</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>

                    <!-- Added rows (useful for ad/landing-page visitors) -->
                    <tr>
                        <td>Designed for configuration</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Clear error diagnostics</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Safe for large configs</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quick Start -->
    <div class="try-under-a-minute full-bleed pr-8">
        <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl pt-1 pb-6">
            <h2>⚡ Try YINI in under a minute</h2>
            <blockquote>No build step. No installation. No setup.</blockquote>
            <ul class="space-y-6">
                <li>
                    Save the two lines below as <code>config.yini</code>: <i
                    ></i><br />
                    <pre><code class="language-yini">{`^ App
name = "Hello"`}</code></pre>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>From your terminal, run this:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">npx yini-cli parse --pretty config.yini</code></pre>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>Results in JSON:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
    "App": {
        "name": "Hello"
    }
}`}</code></pre>
                </li>
            </ul>
            That's it.
        </section>
    </div>

    <section class="pb-0">
        <h2>🧪 Real example</h2>
        <h3>YINI code:</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="./samples/configuration.yini.png"
                ><img
                    class="mt-0 mb-1 rounded-lg border-2 border-black/90"
                    src="./samples/configuration.yini.png"
                    alt="YINI Configuration File"
                /></a
            >
            Source file: <a href="./samples/configuration.yini" target="_blank"
                >configuration.yini</a
            >
        </p>
        <!-- <pre><code class="language-yini">{`/* YINI Configuration File */

^ App
name = "My App"
version = "1.2.3"
debug = off          // Predictable boolean
pageSize = 25

^^ Server            // Sub-section of App
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 8080
allowedOrigins = ["https://myapp.com", "http://localhost:3000"]

^^ Features
enableSearch = true
experimental = ["new-ui", "streaming-api"]

# This is also a valid comment style

^^ Database
engine = "postgres"
ssl = true
pool = { min: 5, max: 25 }  # Inline object
`}</code></pre> -->

        <h3>Parsed output (JavaScript object):</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-js">{`{
  App: {
    name: 'My App',
    version: '1.2.3',
    debug: false,
    pageSize: 25,
    Server: {
      host: '127.0.0.1',
      port: 8080,
      allowedOrigins: [ 'https://myapp.com', 'http://localhost:3000' ]
    },
    Features: { enableSearch: true, experimental: [ 'new-ui', 'streaming-api' ] },
    Database: { engine: 'postgres', ssl: true, pool: { min: 5, max: 25 } }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
            New to YINI? Read a short <a
                href="/intro-yini-config-format"
                class="underline">intro to the YINI format</a
            >.
        </p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <section>
        <h2>🔭 Next steps</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                {
                    /*
                    STEP:    Get Started
                    PURPOSE: Immediate action
